  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1258 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nHeat continues to be the main talking point for the extended long-\nterm period as model agreement does not see the the upper-level\nhigh pressure over the Panhandles laying off anytime soon. With\nthis high-pressure in place, the Panhandles will hold onto a more\nstagnate and dry upper-level pattern that will aid in forcing\ntemperatures near or over the triple digit mark through the end of\nJuly. Obviously such hot temperatures over multiple consecutive\ndays rather typical for this time of year. However, what could\nmake this much more dangerous is that low temperatures are not\nfairing much better with present expectations seeing locations,\nsuch as Amarillo and Dalhart, have the potential to either break\nor tie their record warm low temperature each night. Expectations\nlike these would also mean that there is very little relief\noccurring during overnight hours which may only amplify the risk\nof seeing heat related illness as each day passes. One thing that\nmay be our saving grace over the course of next week, however, is\nthe potential to see some shift in the upper-level high pressure.\nAny shift in said high could lead to moisture pushing in and\ncreating cloud cover or isolated storms that can provide a bit of\nrelief to the Panhandles. A few of the ensemble have even\nsuggested the potential of some northwesterly flow that could\nforce storms off the Southern Range starting Wednesday afternoon.\nStill given the present uncertainty for activity, it is becoming\nincreasingly likely that the expected heat will primary concern\nfor the week with the potential for long lasting heat related\nproducts becoming more likely by the day.",
